  "text_sample": "Your Page Title Go to homepage M\u00f8lle \u00ae Overview Buy M\u00f8lle Skog Studio, 24 m\u00b2 \u20ac68,000 Sj\u00f6 One bedroom, 48 m\u00b2 \u20ac124,000 Lia Two storey, 86 m\u00b2 \u20ac186,000 Kust Twin gable, 132 m\u00b2 \u20ac248,000 All homes Overview Buy M\u00f8lle Close Homes How it works Pricing Configure Visit a cabin More Add-ons Customers About Journal Talk to us Enquire Contact [email protected] A week in the hall in Mora Eighteen carpenters, one cradle per cabin and a stove that never goes out. How a M\u00f8lle is built indoors. Published 6 June 2025 All entries The hall in Mora used to be a sawmill. It still smells like one. Eighteen carpenters work here, and on most weeks there are four cabins on the floor at different stages. Monday, the frame Every cabin starts on a steel cradle that it never leaves until crane day. The spruce frame goes up first, walls and floor together, from timber sawn less than two hours away. Because nothing is ever rained on, nothing swells or twists. Tuesday and Wednesday, the shell Wood fibre insulation goes into the walls, then the oak windows, then the roof. By Wednesday evening the cabin is closed, and the work moves inside. Thursday, the inside Pine boards on the walls and ceilings, oiled with linseed",
